WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.160
Follow Langfocus on Facebook, Twitter, and Instagram.

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:17.300
Hello, everyone. Welcome to the Langfocus channel 
and my name is Paul.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:22.680
Today's topic is the Malay language, 
or "Bahasa Melayu" as it's called in Malay.

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:26.260
People outside of Southeast Asia 
may not be very familiar with Malay,

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:33.080
but if we include all speakers of all varieties of Malay, it is 
one of the top ten most widely spoken languages in the world

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:36.640
And when I say widely, 
I mean by a large number of people.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:42.480
It has around 77 million native speakers 
and around 173 million second language speakers

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.560
for a total of about 250 million speakers.

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:48.230
Of course, different sources give different numbers,
but something like that.

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:54.690
It's spoken in Malaysia where it has around 18.5 million 
native speakers out of a population of around 32 million people

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:59.540
Malaysia is an ethnically and linguistically diverse place 
with large communities of people speaking Chinese

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:03.340
and Indian languages as well as
 numerous indigenous minority languages.

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:05.660
Everyone in Malaysia now learns Malay at school.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:08.820
But some speakers of other languages
either aren't fluent in Malay

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:12.299
or they choose not to speak it very much 
after they graduate from school.

00:01:12.299 --> 00:01:15.599
Singapore's another ethnically diverse
place where Malays are a minority.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.700
The Malay language is spoken by around
700,000 people there or 13 % of the population .

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:27.560
It is also spoken by around 266 thousand people 
or about 64% of the population of Brunei.

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:30.280
What TF? It's not broon-EYE it's Brunei, idiot!

00:01:30.479 --> 00:01:32.939
Oh yeah sorry, in English, we say Brunei.

00:01:32.939 --> 00:01:38.059
- Oh my god, you slaughtered the word Brunei haha.
- Ok, uh... Brunei.

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:44.340
So like I was saying it's spoken by around 266 thousand people 
or about 64% of the population of Brunei.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:49.120
It is also spoken by around 1 million people, 
in southern Thailand adjacent to Peninsular Malaysia.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:53.040
And Malay is spoken by around 
11.23 million people in Indonesia

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:58.660
But that's only part of the story. There were also about 
43 million native speakers of Bahasa Indonesia

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:02.760
and around a hundred and fifty-five (million) 
second language speakers of Bahasa Indonesia.

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:04.400
So, what's Bahasa Indonesia?

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.399
Well, when speaking of the Malay language, 
we need to distinguish between Malay dialects,

00:02:08.399 --> 00:02:12.079
which vary from place to place 
and the standardized forms of the language.

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:17.400
The first standardized form of the language is Bahasa 
Melayu which is used in Malaysia, Brunei and Singapore

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:21.460
and functions as an umbrella language 
for all of the malay dialects that are spoken there.

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:24.500
The second standardized form of the language 
is Bahasa Indonesia

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:29.180
and is used in Indonesia and functions as a lingua franca 
between speakers of different languages

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:31.320
because there are hundreds of languages in Indonesia.

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:35.320
I plan to focus specifically on Indonesian
in a different video in the future.

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:39.800
This is interesting: in Malaysia, 
the official name of Malay is "Bahasa Malaysia",

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:44.700
meaning the "language of Malaysia" instead of 
"Bahasa Melayu" which means the "language of the Malays"

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:50.440
This name was chosen to show that the language 
is a language for all Malaysian citizens
and not only for ethnic Malays.

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:53.620
But some people want to change the name 
back to Bahasa Melayu.

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:54.300
History

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:58.380
Malay is the most widely spoken language
in the Austronesian language family.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:02.340
The Austronesian languages can most likely
be traced back to a common ancestor

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:06.280
Proto-Austronesian, in what is 
now Taiwan around 5200 years ago.

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:08.700
Various dialects arose from Proto-Austronesian.

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:13.180
One of them being Proto-Malayo-Polynesian 
which began splitting up into various languages,

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:17.720
around 4,000 years ago as Austronesian people
expanded to the South from Taiwan.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:22.380
The Malayo-Polynesian sub-branch includes 
most of the Austronesian languages outside of Taiwan.

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:25.800
A Proto-Malayic language, 
the ancestor of all Malay dialects,

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:29.640
probably originated on the island of Borneo 
more  than 2,000 years ago,

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:33.440
before its speakers migrated West to Sumatra 
and the Malay Peninsula

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:34.100
Old Malay.

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:40.360
The beginning of the Common Era and by that I mean 
after the year 1 CE or 1AD - same thing by the way -

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:45.220
was a time of great Indian influence in the region
And, during this time, Old Malay emerged.

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:49.620
It was highly influenced by sanskrit and
written in Indian scripts like Pallava

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:54.260
The oldest known incriptions of old Malay
are the Kebukan Bukit, which were found in Sumatra

00:03:54.269 --> 00:03:56.609
and dig back to the seventh century CE.

00:03:56.609 --> 00:04:00.900
The oldest thought Malay manuscript 
that still exists today is that Tanjung Tanah.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:05.260
A legal text from the 14th century, 
still before Islam was widespread in the area.

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:06.290
Classical Malay.

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:10.540
Islam soon became dominant in the area
and trade with the Muslim world increased,

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:14.320
resulting in a lot of Arabic and Persian vocabulary
entering the language

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:16.980
and resulting in the adoption of the Jawi script

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:21.050
Jawi is a version of the Arabic script
that was modified to suit the Malay language

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:24.220
During the Malacca Sultanate,
which was like a kingdom,

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:29.120
and its successor the Johor Sultanate,
Malay became widely used as a lingua franca.

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:36.380
During this time, Islamic literature greatly affected 
the language, with more Arabic and Persian vocabulary 
entering Malay

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:40.760
And because Malacca was a busy international port 
with people from various countries,

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:45.280
Malay also absorbed vocabulary from
different languages like Tamil and Chinese languages.

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:49.720
During this Classical Malay Period,
Malay evolved into something relatively similar

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:52.480
to the Bahasa Melayu and Bahasa Indonesia of today.

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:56.440
There were actually two forms of Malay 
that became widespread during this time period.

00:04:56.440 --> 00:05:01.440
First, there was the formal Classical Malay 
which was the state language 
and the language of courts and literature.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:04.100
And there was Bazaar Malay or Melayu Pasar

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:08.200
a low language spoken by traders 
as a lingua franca throughout the archipelago.

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:12.400
Bazaar Malay is thought to have been a pidgin language 
featuring vocabulary from Malay

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:15.420
but influenced by other languages,
such as  Chinese languages.

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:18.800
And there were also local Malay dialects 
that varied from place to place.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:19.880
Pre-modern Malay.

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:23.640
In the 19th century, European colonial powers 
were dominating the region,

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:26.360
with the Dutch in Indonesia 
and the British in Malaysia.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:33.240
During this time period, improvements to the printing press 
made Malay books and newspapers more widely 
available to the common people.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:38.640
So that, literary Malay or Classical Malay was no longer 
just the language of the courts and the elite.

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:43.340
The literary Malay of the Malacca and the Johor Sultanates
- in other words, Classical Malay -

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:47.380
was recognized by the British and by the Dutch 
as the standard form of the language

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:49.080
and became the language of Education.

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:53.580
One notable change during the colonial period 
was the adoption of the Rumi writing system,

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:57.100
the use of Roman characters to write Malay,
instead of the Jawi Script.

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:03.100
And English loanwords began to trickle into Malay,
in the areas under British control.

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:04.000
Modern Malay

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:08.740
The abundance of Malay literature in the 19th century 
sparks new enthusiasm for the language.

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:12.880
And Malay linguists began their efforts to
standardize and modernize the language.

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:18.340
In 1936, the Malay scholar Za'aba
published a series of books called "Pelita Bahasa"

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:24.380
in which he updated the grammar of Classical Malay,
forming the basis of Bahasa Melayu and Bahasa Indonesia

00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:28.200
In both Malaysia and Indonesia, the standard form 
of Malay became an important part

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:32.000
of their respective nationalist movements 
and their struggles for independence.

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:37.620
When Indonesia declared independence in 1945,
Bahasa Indonesia became the national language

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:42.580
and, when Malaysia became independent in 1957, 
Bahasa Melayu became the national language.

00:06:42.700 --> 00:06:43.480
Dialects.

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:48.280
Bahasa Melayu is the standard form of Malay 
used in Malaysia and Singapore and Brunei.

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:52.820
And it's the form of the language taught in schools 
but there are numerous spoken dialects of Malay

00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:57.140
that vary not only from country to country 
but also from state to state and from town to town.

00:06:57.140 --> 00:06:59.660
With differences in pronunciation and vocabulary.

00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:04.900
The Malay spoken and Sabah and Sarawak tends to 
be more similar to that spoken in Brunei and Singapore

00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:07.020
but different from the dialects in West Malaysia.

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:10.540
And some states in West Malaysia are known 
for being quite divergent

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:12.420
For example, the dialect of Kelantan.

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:16.020
Kelantan borders Thailand to the north
where a similar dialect is spoken.

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:19.380
The difference is that in Kelantan,
everyone learned standard Malay at school

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:22.060
but, in southern Thailand,
everyone learns Thai at school.

00:07:22.190 --> 00:07:26.010
This makes cross dialect communication
harder for speakers of Malay in Thailand.

00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:30.040
But, for speakers of other Malay dialect, 
standard Malay is a unifying force

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:33.840
and speaking a little more formally
can help people bridge communication gaps.

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:37.980
Standard Malay is also the form of Malay 
learned by non-Malays at school.

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:41.360
So Standard Malay is sometimes
the language of inter-ethnic communication.

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:43.460
Though some people choose to speak English instead.

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:44.820
So, what is Malay like?

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:48.020
Like most Austronesian languages,
Malay is not a tonal language.

00:07:48.020 --> 00:07:52.980
It has simple phonology and every written character 
represents only one sound in most cases.

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:55.300
It has a simple SVO sentence structure.

00:07:55.300 --> 00:07:57.820
For example:

00:07:57.960 --> 00:07:59.540
meaning "I eat vegetables"

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:02.840
I don't eat meat

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:04.320
Now, let's add a modal verb.

00:08:07.010 --> 00:08:08.270
I don't want to eat meat

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:14.000
I don't want to eat meat or drink milk.

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:17.920
Malay grammar is simple 
First of all, there's no grammatical gender.

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:21.920
There are also no noun cases.

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:25.880
There is also no plural form in Malay.

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:30.380
If you want to make a plural explicit,
you re-duplicate the word or repeat the word.

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:32.400
"Orang" means a person

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:34.720
"orang-orang" means "people".

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:37.060
And, speaking of orang : fun fact !

00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:40.940
"orang" means "a person" in Malay.
"hutan" means "forest" in Malay.

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:45.880
"orang hutan" means "forest person",
also known as orangutan, cool!

00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:48.960
That's the etymology of the word "orangutan", but also 
in Malay the animal's name is "orang utan" with no "h"

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:53.040
If you want to modify a noun, 
you add the modifying word after the noun.

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:55.300
The word for book: buku

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:59.320
Now, let's add a demonstrative pronoun. 
"Buku itu" = "that book"

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:05.840
or with a possessive pronoun 
"buku saya" = "my book" 
or "buku buku saya" = "my books"

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:09.720
or with an adjective
"buku mahal" = "an expensive book"

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:13.680
In Malay, there is no copula verb (to be )
at least in regular speech.

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:19.340
And there is no definite or indefinite article. 
So "buku mahal" could also mean "the book is expensive"

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:22.400
But to be more clear, you might say "Buku itu mahal"

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:29.800
Prepositions in Malay are very straightforward and intuitive
... = "I come from Canada"

00:09:32.660 --> 00:09:34.420
= "I want to go to the night market"

00:09:36.980 --> 00:09:40.600
"There is a discount for students".
In this sentence, "Ada" means "there is".

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:44.920
One of the things that makes learning Malay fun
is that there are no verb conjugations

00:09:45.020 --> 00:09:49.900
The form of the verb stays exactly the same regardless 
of whether it's in present tense, past tense or future tense.

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:56.120
So we have the verb meaning to eat. In present tense, 
"makan". In past tense, "makan". In future tense, "makan".

00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:59.320
The tense is determined by specifying a time period like:

00:10:01.240 --> 00:10:04.580
"He will eat later" 
or by using an adverb before the verb:

00:10:06.520 --> 00:10:07.400
he will eat

00:10:09.380 --> 00:10:11.100
"he already ate" ; or

00:10:13.130 --> 00:10:15.550
"he is currently eating" or "he's eating now".

00:10:15.550 --> 00:10:17.370
And there are some other adverbs like this.

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:22.280
But, of course, it's not quite that simple 
because we have the verb "makan", "to eat"

00:10:22.280 --> 00:10:28.300
but we also have the verb "dimakan" = "to be eaten" 
and "termakan" = "to be eaten accidentally"

00:10:28.700 --> 00:10:31.460
And we have the verb "memakan" = "to consume".

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:34.960
Then, we have "memakankan" 
= "to feed something to someone"

00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:38.340
These are examples of one of Malay's
most challenging features for learners:

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:40.900
its system of building words from roots and affixes.

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:44.940
Up above, we created some new verbs
but we can also create other types of words.

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:46.760
food

00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:49.380
someone who eats

00:10:50.780 --> 00:10:52.000
means a meat eater.

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:54.620
Malay makes extensive use of this kind of affixation.

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:58.580
Let's look at one more example using the root word
"ajar" which means "to teach".

00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:00.420
teachings

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:02.200
to learn

00:11:03.500 --> 00:11:04.200
to teach

00:11:05.620 --> 00:11:08.920
"to be taught" (intransitive)
 as in "I'm being taught"

00:11:10.530 --> 00:11:13.010
transitive as in "the lesson is being taught"

00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:15.280
to study

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:17.500
to be studied

00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:19.160
student

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:20.820
teacher

00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:22.640
learning

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:25.320
to be educated

00:11:25.880 --> 00:11:29.680
All of these affixes might seem overwhelming 
at first but they're fairly systematic.

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:32.700
And once you get used to the function
of all of the different affixes,

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:35.820
then it helps you to understand words
that you've never heard before

00:11:35.940 --> 00:11:38.520
and it helps you to guess how to say words 
that you don't know yet.

00:11:38.620 --> 00:11:40.520
That sounds useful!

00:11:40.720 --> 00:11:46.220
Standard Malay is a very user-friendly language for learners, 
especially if you're used to learning more complex languages.

00:11:46.220 --> 00:11:50.040
The biggest challenge that you likely face 
is the difference between the formal language

00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:55.360
and the casual language and the dialectal variation
that you'll encounter. Especially different pronunciation.

00:11:55.440 --> 00:11:59.720
But by learning the standard polite form of Malay,
you form a good basis for communication

00:11:59.720 --> 00:12:03.080
and you can learn the more colloquial style 
of communication as you go.

00:12:03.090 --> 00:12:09.210
Stay tuned for a future episode 
in which I cover  "Bahasa Indonesia"
and I'll focus in on that form of Malay specifically.

